## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical tenancy controls in a NoSQL store; checks how to enforce data isolation with security rules, IAM, and network boundaries; assesses ability to design automated containment.

## Key Concepts
- Firestore security rules and tenant-scoped paths
- Per-tenant service accounts with least privilege
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls
- CI/CD gate with synthetic tenants and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Firestore rules snippet (illustrative)
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /tenants/{tenantId}/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.tenants.contains(tenantId);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for potential rule bypasses in CI?
- What are the trade-offs of using per-tenant collections vs single collection with tenant_id field?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests the ability to design automated containment in a multi-tenant GCP setup with shared VPCs, emphasizing rapid, verifiable rollback and per-tenant boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via IAM Conditions
- Per-tenant VPC Service Controls perimeters
- CMEK rotation and auditability
- Synthetic data for rollback validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy samples (illustrative)
const policy = {
  bindings: [
    {
      role: 'roles/bigquery.dataViewer',
      members: ['user:tenantA@example.com'],
      condition: { title: 'TenantA', expression: "resource.name.startsWith('projects/ORG/datasets/tenant-A/')" }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment under a simulated breach?
- How would changes be audited and rolled back if misconfig detected?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests on-demand, auditable access in a shared GCP workspace, ensuring least privilege and robust revocation.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Conditions

- Access Context Manager

- VPC Service Controls

- Auditability and synthetic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example policy binding (pseudo)
{
  \"bindings\": [
    {
      \"role\": \"roles/bigquery.dataViewer\",
      \"members\": [\"serviceAccount:auditor@example.iam.gserviceaccount.com\"],
      \"condition\": {
        \"title\": \"tenantScope\",
        \"expression\": \"resource.name.startsWith('projects/tenant-123/datasets/tenant_123_') && request.time < timestamp('2026-12-01T00:00:00Z')\"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multiple concurrent audits?
- How do you prove no cross-tenant access occurred during expiry?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to secure public Cloud Run endpoints for multiple partners using standard GCP controls.

## Key Concepts
- Least-privilege IAM per partner
- IAP for authenticating users to Cloud Run
- Per-partner isolation via separate service accounts or gateway policies
- Credential rotation and auditability
- Synthetic tests to validate misconfig denial

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test: simulate non-permitted access and expect HTTP 403
const token = obtainTokenFor('partnerX');
fetch('https://api.example.run.app/endpoint', { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }})
  .then(response => {
    if (response.status === 403) {
      console.log('✓ Access properly blocked for unauthorized partner');
    } else {
      console.error('✗ Security misconfiguration detected');
    }
  });
```
